<!--
    Kuna pushan in-function interpreters: P2 flow-follow, option pushan
    (default auto).  The script loads the x86-64 PIE
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/pushan_interp_x86_64
    (source pushan_interp_x86_64.c, gcc -O0): six functions that are their
    own bytecode interpreters, the shape Tigress Virtualize gives a function,
    each running `s = 0; for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) s += a < i ? i : a;
    return s;` with its virtual PC in a local.  sw_writable dispatches by a
    switch on an opcode byte in writable .data, sw_readonly the same from
    .rodata, th_copied by `goto **pc` through handler addresses it copies
    from writable data into a 32 KiB local with memcpy (after gcc's stack
    probe), th_inline the same with the copy an inline `rep movsq`, wv_woven by a switch through pc[sel] over two programs run
    round-robin (the second a bogus endless loop), and sw_patched by a switch
    over bytecode it patches with its argument.

    Pass 1 (`option pushan off`) is the bug: sw_writable decompiles to its
    interpreter, its virtual PC pointed at sw_code (#1) and a loop around
    the switch on the fetched opcode, with no trace of the virtual program.
    Pass 2 (the default) finds each interpreter after the plain flow showed a
    computed jump: six devirtualized headers (#2).  The fetches through the
    VPC read the file's bytes; the five functions whose bytecode is in
    writable memory say the body assumes it is what the file holds, and
    sw_readonly does not (#3).  The virtual loop and branch come back in the
    five complete programs (#4, #5, #6); the memcpy stays a call, and the
    `rep movsq` that only the folded fetches read is left out, so no copy
    loop remains (#7, #10), the woven function follows its first program and
    says so (#8), and
    sw_patched, whose patched opcode decides a dispatch, is reported
    incomplete (#9).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
